.TH LOCALPASSKEYD 8 "October 2026" "localpasskey 0.2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
localpasskeyd \- software FIDO2/WebAuthn authenticator that stores passkeys on this computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B localpasskeyd
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-state\-dir
.IR directory ]
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B localpasskeyd
turns the computer into a passkey authenticator, similar to Windows Hello.
It is normally started by
.BR systemd (1)
as
.BR localpasskey.service .
.PP
The daemon creates a virtual USB security key through
.IR /dev/uhid .
Browsers and
.BR libfido2 (3)
programs talk to it over the FIDO CTAP2 protocol exactly as they would to a
hardware key. systemd's
.B fido_id
udev rules give the user of the active desktop session access to the
device node.
.PP
For each request the daemon finds the process that opened the device and
uses that user's passkeys. Before a passkey is created or used, a polkit
authentication dialog appears in that user's session and asks for the
user's own password (or anything else PAM accepts, such as a fingerprint).
Browsers that ask for user verification receive it, because the
authenticator advertises built-in user verification.
.PP
Browsers can also list and delete passkeys from their security-key settings
(CTAP credential management) once the user has set a PIN. The PIN alone is
not enough: the polkit dialog appears before a browser may manage passkeys,
and a passkey deleted this way goes to the trash, from which
.BR localpasskey (1)
can restore it. Eight wrong PINs in a row block the PIN, three in a row
block further attempts until the service restarts.
.PP
A user can have any number of passkeys, including several accounts for the
same site; for sign-ins without a user name the browser shows an account
chooser. Passkeys are device-bound: they are not synchronised or backed up
anywhere.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read the configuration from
.I file
instead of
.IR /etc/localpasskey/localpasskeyd.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " directory"
Store passkeys in
.IR directory .
Defaults to
.B $STATE_DIRECTORY
or
.IR /var/lib/localpasskey .
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Listen for
.BR localpasskey (1)
on
.IR path .
Defaults to
.IR control.sock
in
.B $RUNTIME_DIRECTORY
or
.IR /run/localpasskey .
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log debug messages, including the names of the sites involved.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/localpasskey/localpasskeyd.conf
Configuration: confirmation timeout, desktop notifications, log level and
the USB IDs of the virtual device.
.B notifications
turns all desktop notifications on or off;
.B activity_notifications
controls the ones shown when a passkey is saved, used, moved to the trash or
permanently deleted, as opposed
to those about declined, timed-out or failed requests.
.TP
.I /var/lib/localpasskey/users/UID/credentials.json
The passkeys of the user with that uid, including those in the trash.
Readable by root only; private keys are additionally encrypted with
.IR /var/lib/localpasskey/master.key .
Back up both files together, or neither.
.TP
.I /var/lib/localpasskey/users/UID/pin.json
A hash of that user's PIN, encrypted with the same master key, and the
number of attempts left. Present only while a PIN is set.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.localpasskey.policy
polkit actions
.B org.localpasskey.authenticate
(create or use a passkey) and
.B org.localpasskey.manage
(move passkeys to the trash, restore or permanently delete them, and set
or remove the PIN).
.SH SECURITY
Approving the dialog is the only thing that stops a website from using
your passkeys, so do not grant
.B org.localpasskey.authenticate
without authentication in polkit rules.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR localpasskey (1),
.BR localpasskey-bridge (1),
.BR polkit (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
